<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Anga</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 540 BCE (approx.) · capital Champa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An eastern kingdom with its capital at the river port of Champa, the first great conquest of Magadha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Anga lay east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/champa">Champa</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagalpur">Bhagalpur</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. It is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> as a distant eastern people and by the 6th century BCE it was listed among the sixteen mahajanapadas. Buddhist texts say Anga once dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, and a king named Brahmadatta is said to have defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara's</a> father.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/champa">Champa</a> was counted among the six great cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>'s day. It was a river port from which merchants were said to sail to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suphannaphum">Suvarnabhumi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and its wealth rested on trade and the farming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>. Little is known of its government beyond the names of a few kings in Buddhist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> tradition.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 540 BCE the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a> conquered Anga, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/champa">Champa</a> was governed for a time by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>. The conquest began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha's</a> rise to dominance over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-anga.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Anga" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Vedic_Culture_(1100-500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 540 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Champa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Jainism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anga, Anga Mahajanapada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-620&amp;focus=kingdom-of-anga" aria-label="Kingdom of Anga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6984 1249 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Anga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7304 1505L7266 1513"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7266 1513h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7304 1505h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7304 1505h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-620&amp;focus=kingdom-of-anga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

